<h1>path_get_x</h1>
<p>With this function you can get the x coordinate of a position on any given path. 0 is the start of the path, 1 is the end of the path, and anything in between equates to that far through the path. This needn&#39;t be a defining point of the path, it can be anywhere on it.</p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Syntax:</h4>
<p class="code">path_get_x(ind, pos);</p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th>Argument</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>index</td>
<td><span data-keyref="Type_Asset_Path"><a target="_blank" href="../../../../The_Asset_Editors/Paths.htm">Path Asset</a></span></td>
<td>The index of the path to check.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>pos</td>
<td><span data-keyref="Type_Real"><a target="_blank" href="../../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">Real</a></span></td>
<td>How far through the path to check. Between 0 (start) and 1 (end).</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p> </p>
<h4>Returns:</h4>
<p class="code"><span data-keyref="Type_Real"><a target="_blank" href="../../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">Real</a></span></p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Example:</h4>
<p class="code">x = path_get_x(pth_WallWalk, 0.5);</p>
<p>This will set the calling instance&#39;s x to the x coordinate of the point at exactly halfway through the given path.</p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p>
